## 1. Introduction
The dominant graphite and metal oxide anode materials for lithium-ion batteries are out of operation for sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) due to the sluggish reaction kinetics from much larger $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ radius (1.02 Å versus $0.76\mathrm{~\AA~}$ of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ) [1–4]. Thus, it is extremely urgent to exploit vigorous anode materials with high specific capacity and long cycling life for high-energy SIBs. Transitionmetal compounds, especially for nickel phosphides/sulfides/sele nides, have drawn great concern because of their favorable conversion reaction, which can produce high theoretical capacity (the theoretical values of ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}_{:}$ $\mathrm{Ni}S_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ are 1333, 873 and 495 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively), low redox potential, and good electrochemical reversibility [5–8]. However, these anode materials also suffer from weak electroconductibility, drastic structural collapse, and huge volume variation (ranging from $490\%$ to $525\%$ ) upon repetitive dis/-charge processes [9–12]. Constructing controllable nanostructures has demonstrated an effective strategy to alleviate above-mentioned problems.
The well-defined yolk-shell nanostructure is emerging as the most promising alternative in electrode material fabrication because of its confinement effect and robust structural stability [4,13,14]. The unique yolk-shell framework can serve as a favourable platform to introduce some functional groups by surface modification [15]. Additionally, the outside shell could alleviate the aggregation and volume variation of active material [16–18]. Therefore, it is highly required to develop effective approach to fabricate electrode materials with yolk-shell structure. However, the traditional synthesis methods are mainly limited by the sacrificial template technology, depending on silica or metal oxide hard templates and corrosive acid or alkali etching, inevitably leading to structure and component destruction as well as energy consumption and environment pollution [10,17,19]. The development of effective synthesis strategy to fabricate yolk-shell electrode materials still remains challenging. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as organic–inorganic hybrid materials are fabricated by organic ligands and metal ions or clusters via coordination bonds [20]. Various MOFs structures can be constructed by flexibly regulating species of metal ions and/or ligands, displaying complex components, highly porosity, large surface area, adjustable pore size, abundant active sites. However, the poor electrical conductivity of MOFs seriously limits their applications particularly in the field of electrocatalyst and battery. Fortunately, MOFs can be selected as precursors or templates to be in situ converted into carbon-encapsulated metallic composites with uniformly dispersed $\mathrm{non}/$ -metal elements through anion exchange strategy without introducing additional carbon material [21]. Moreover, MOFs derivatives can well inherit the original morphology of MOFs precursors/templates [22]. The in situ generated carbon layer can not only improve the conductivity, but also maintain the integrity and prevent side reactions [23]. Besides, MOF-derived strategy can avoid many disadvantages in traditional template method that is easy to waste materials and destroy the skeleton in the process of acid pickling and etching the template, such as silicon. Therefore, it is reasonable to obtain the yolk-shell nickelic spheres from MOFs precursors.
Herein, we report a general MOF-derived route via Ostwald ripening to in situ fabricate hierarchical carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell nickelic spheres. Through engineering the structures of MOFs, the carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C})$ , $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}/\mathsf{C})$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(NiSe_{2}/C)}$ spheres are prepared by subsequent anionization reaction. This novel synthetic strategy is a simple, feasible and universal synthetic method, which is not only limited to the synthesis of nickel compounds, but also can be used to synthesize other carbon-contained metal compounds. These unique hierarchical architectures can integrate nano/-microstructural features to offer multiple advantages for maintaining structural stability and enriching crystal boundaries with sufficient electrochemical active sites. Based on the above unique skeleton and physchemical characteristics, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ anode delivers much higher reversible capacity $(765.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ ) and superior rate capability than that of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ electrodes. These results further indicate that metal phosphide can exhibit better sodium storage properties in comparison with sulfide and selenide counterparts. The phys/-chemical properties of nickel phosphate as well as nickel sulfide and selenide counterparts have been expounded in detail. This innovative approach should provide a scientific basis for in situ synthesis of carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell transitionmetal electrodes for application in advanced energy storage devices.
## 2. Experimental section
The practical Chemical reagents of trimesic acid $(98\%)$ , $\mathsf{N a H}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{PO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(99\%)$ , polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, M.W. 24000, K23-27), ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $(98\%)$ , S $(99.95\%)$ and Se $(99.9\%)$ come from Aladdin.
## 2.1. Synthesis of the Ni-MOFs spheres
$_{0.15\mathrm{~\scriptsize~g~}}$ trimesic acid, $1.033\textrm{\ g}$ polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP $\mathsf{M}=58000$ , K29-32) and $0.432\textrm{g N i}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solvent (the volume ratio of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and DMF is 1:1). Then the homogeneously mixed solution was transferred into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and kept in oven under $160^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The prepared gramineous product was washed with deionized water three times, then followed by anhydrous ethanol for three times. The washed samples were placed in oven for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $80~^{\circ}C$ .
## 2.2. Synthesis of the yolk-shell carbon-encapsulated $N i_{2}P$ composites
The obtained Ni-MOFs and $\Nu\mathsf{a}\Nu_{2}\mathsf{P}0_{2}.\Nu_{2}0$ (the weight ratio of 1:10) were put in two quartz boats, where the Ni-MOFs and $\mathsf{N a H}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{P O}_{2}.\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ power were placed at the middle and upstream of the tube furnace in argon atmosphere. The final yolk-shell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres were obtained after thermal treatment at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of the yolk-shell carbon-encapsulated $N i S_{2}$ composites
The obtained Ni-MOFs and S power (the weight ratio of 1:10) were treated in argon atmosphere at $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a heating rate of $1\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ \mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of the yolk-shell carbon-encapsulated $N i S e_{2}$ composites
The obtained Ni-MOFs and Se power (the weight ratio of 1:10) were treated in argon atmosphere at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with a heating rate of $2\ {\overset{\circ}{\subset}}\ \operatorname*{min}^{-1}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
The carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell nickelic spheres were prepared via MOF-derived route as shown in Scheme 1. The nickel nitrate, trimesic acid ligand and polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) surfactant were first mixed thoroughly and then sealed into Teflon reactor to prepare Ni-MOFs spheres by hydrothermal method. The detailed synthesis mechanism can be explained as the following two steps. At the beginning of hydrothermal reaction, the nickelic nanocrystallines are present and gradually agglomerate into solid spherical assembly driven by surface free energy. As the aging time increases, the solid spherical aggregations would undergo a hollowing process. The nucleation mechanism can be explained by Ostwald ripening theory. Specifically, mass transport via Ostwald ripening has been proved to be a moderate strategy to generate interior spaces [24,25]. The driving force of Ostwald ripening is the reduction of total surface free energy. Lots of tiny nickelic crystallites nucleate from solution and rapidly grow into solid spheres with larger size, leading to the decrease of specific surface energy in per unit mass and the total free energy. However, the inner crystallites possess a comparatively higher surface energy and can be dissolved and transfer out to generate channels connecting inner space and outer space in the MOF shells [26]. Thus the interior spaces can be created in terms of matter state and agglomerating pattern of pristine crystallites. Finally, the homogeneous core–shell nanostructures with same chemical constituent are successfully fabricated. The actual morphological evolution of Ni-MOFs precursor is shown in Fig. S1. Except for simple carbonation, the Ni species in MOFs precursors with different structures (solid spheres, yolk-shell spheres and hollow spheres) were separately converted into corresponding porous ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ , $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ spheres by phosphorization, sulfuration and selenation process, encapsulated by the outer thin carbon layer from carbonized organic ligand (Fig. S2). The unchanged structure demonstrates that the thermal treatment has no effect on morphology inheritance. The architectural design for the interior space via MOFs template can be attained through adjusting the experiment parameter, and could be generalized to synthesize other transition-metal materials.
The crystal phases and chemical valences of obtained carbonencapsulated nickelic spheres were checked by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis. In Fig. 1a, the peaks at $40.8^{\circ}$ , $44.6^{\circ}$ , $47.4^{\circ}$ , $54.3^{\circ}$ and $54.9^{\circ}$ are indexed to the (111), (201), (210), (300) and (211) planes of hexagonal ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ crystal (P321(150), PDF card 03–0953). The lattice constant $\tt{a}=2.22\tt{\AA}$ in (111) plane is calculated by the Bragg’s formula $\left(\mathsf{d}=0.5\lambda/\sin(\theta)\right)$ . In Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 1b), both two major peaks of Ni 2p3/2 and Ni 2p1/2 are fitted into three peaks of the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (852.3/869.5 eV), $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ (854.8/872.4 eV) and oxidized $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (858.5/875.3 eV) [27,28]. In $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ spectrum (Fig. 1c), there are two kinds of phosphorus species. The fitted peaks at 128.9 and $129.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ are indicated as $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of Ni-P bonds, and the peaks at 133.1 and $134.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ of PAO bonds, which may come from the oxidized $\mathrm{\bf~P}$ species as the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}@\mathsf{C}$ exposed to air [29,30]. As the counterpart, the cubic $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ crystal shows the $\tt{a}=\bar{2}.84\mathrm{~\AA~}$ to (200) plane in Pa-3(205) group (PDF card 11–0099, Fig. 1d). The peaks at 856.7, 861.7, and
$864.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ represent the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ and oxidized $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ for Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , and peaks of 168.6 and $169.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ signify the $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ of $S_{2}^{2-}$ for $\mathsf{S2p}$ spectrum in $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ sample (Fig. 1e and f) [31–33]. In Fig. 1g, the cubic $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ crystal shows the same $P a-3(205)$ group with $\tt a=2.69\AA$ in (210) plane (PDF card 41–1495). There are still three fitted peaks of the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (853.1 eV), $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ ( $856.4~\mathrm{eV})$ and oxidized $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ (860.8 eV) for Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ in Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 1h), and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ $^{\prime}52.9\mathrm{~\eV)}$ , Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ $(53.8~\mathrm{\eV})$ , and $\mathsf{S e O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ species $(57.4/58.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ in Se 3d spectrum (Fig. 1i) [34,35]. Besides, the C 1 s spectrum can be fitted the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C},\mathsf{N i}{-}0{-}\mathsf{C}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (Fig. S3) [36]. The electronic shuttling bridge of $_{\mathrm{Ni-O-C}}$ bonds could promote the electronic migration during dis/-charge process [17,37].
These nickelic composites also show high Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) specific surface areas, as shown in Fig. S4. The yolkshell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres exhibit a typical type-IV isotherm with BET specific surface area of $63.74~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is larger than that of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ $(29.24\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1};$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ $(23.11~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ ) spheres. The comparison of BET data of the related Ni-based materials in previous literatures is shown in Table S1-3. The large specific surface area could enlarge effective contact area of electrolyte/active material to improve sodium storage capacity [38,39]. Furthermore, the mass fractions of nickelic compounds and carbon in corresponding carbon-encapsulated nickelic composites were calculated based on the thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA, Fig. S5), respectively. Afterwards, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ composite displays a higher carbon content $(22.8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ than that of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ ( $13.6~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ ( $11.8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ . The specific calculation process for three kinds of nickelic composites is shown in supporting information.
The morphologies of Ni-MOFs and nickelic spheres were observed by Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). The distinct yolk-shell Ni-MOFs precursor is uniformly distributed with the average diameter of roughly $2.32~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S6a and b). Fig. 2a and b show the similar yolk-shell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres $(\sim1.69~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ to the MOFs precursor, with the uniform distribution of Ni and P elements. The line scans analysis (Fig. 2c) reveals that Ni, P elements are mainly distributed on the center and outside of sphere, suggests that the yolk-shell structure are successfully fabricated. As for the $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ samples (Fig. 2d-h), they still possess yolk-shell spheres with the uniform dispersion of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{S}$ , and Ni/Se elements, respectively, and this feasible method of synthesizing yolk-shell spheres is further demonstrated by line scan analysis (Fig. 2f and i). The thermal treatment (phosphorization, sulfuration or selenation) has little effects on the overall morphology evolution but generates a significant impact on particle size distribution, and micro-morphology formation on the nickelic spheres surface. The carbon-encapsulated nickelic spheres exhibit nearly identical average diameter, which is slightly smaller than that of the Ni-MOFs precursor (Fig. S6b-e). The high-temperature treatment can cause water evaporation, structure shrinkage and crystal reconstruction, resulting in the reduction of particle size after phosphating/sulfidation/selenization. Moreover, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ sphere surface is quite smooth, but the surfaces of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres are feather-like and rod-like, respectively. This may be attributed to the Kirkendall effect [40,41], the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ cations and $\mathsf{X}^{x-}$ anions would transfer to the boundary at different migration rates to build the shell, which is promoted by diverse activity of relative movements.
The microstructures of nickelic spheres were further verified by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis. The typical yolkshell Ni-MOFs spheres with average diameter of $2.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ are observed with amorphous state (Fig. S7). In Fig. 3a, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ displays obvious yolk-shell spheres, being well in accordance with SEM results. The lattice fringes with d-spacing of 0.222, 0.194 and $0.203\ \mathrm{nm}$ are indexed to the (111), (210) and (201) planes in hexagonal ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ crystal (Fig. 3b), respectively. The ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ spheres are wrapped by the outer carbon layer (the thickness of $1.32\:\mathrm{nm}$ ), this layer not only could improve electrical conductivity but also divide the active nanoparticles into single unit to avoid side reaction [42]. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) confirms that ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ crystal has good crystallinity, and the highangle annular-dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) and energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping (Fig. 3c and Fig. S8a) further display that Ni, P and C elements are evenly distributed on the surface of yolkshell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ sphere. For $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ sample (Fig. 3d and e), the yolkshell spheres are wrapped by carbon layer $(\sim1.72\ \mathrm{nm})$ with the uniform distribution of Ni, S and C elements (Fig. 3f and Fig. S8b), and d-spacing of 0.282, 0.201 and $0.234~\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to (200), (220) and (211) planes were measured in cubic $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ phase. Similarly, the cubic $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ crystal displays the (2 1 1), (2 2 1)
and (220) planes to d-spacing of 0.244, 0.198 and $0.211{\mathrm{nm}}$ , which are wrapped in carbon layer $1.46\mathrm{nm})$ , with the homogeneous distribution of Ni, Se and C elements (Fig. 3g–i and Fig. S8c). The simultaneous migration of cations/anions towards the carbon matrix under ultrasonicated treatment brings about rich crystal boundaries (Fig. S9), thus can increase electroactive sites to facilitate the deep redox reactions. These SEM and TEM results strongly manifest that MOF-derived strategy is effective way to in situ fabricate carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell materials.
To investigate the electrochemical behaviors of synthesized nickelic composites, the Cyclic Voltammogram (CV) and galvanostatic dis/-charge test were implemented in half-cell SIBs. Fig. 4a shows the first three cycles profiles with a voltage window of $0.05–3\mathrm{~V~}$ at $0.1\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ for $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ sample. In first cathodic scan, the peaks of $0.88{\mathrm{V}}$ and $_{0.42\mathrm{~V~}}$ denote the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion, the reduction of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ and the generation of solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}P}+3\mathrm{Na}^{+}+3\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Na_{3}P})$ . The peak at $1.43~\mathrm{V}$ in first anodic scan indicates the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ extraction, and the oxidation of metallic Ni $(2\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Na_{3}P}\rightarrow\mathrm{Ni_{2}P}+3\mathrm{Na^{+}+3e^{-})}$ [43]. Furthermore, the cathodic peaks of $0.94/0.53\ \mathrm{~V~}$ in the second cycle and $0.85/0.66\mathrm{~V~}$ in the third cycle denote the the reduction of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ ， and anodic peaks indicate the reversible oxidation of Ni, respectively. Obviously, compared with the cathodic peaks position in the first cycle, the cathodic peaks in the subsequent laps have shifted to high potential, due to the drastic structural modifcation in first discharge reaction [10]. Moreover, the decreased closed curves areas after second lap indicate the attenuation of initial specific capacity, resulting from the generation of SEI film and irreversible side effects [17,44].
For the corresponding galvanostatic dis/-charge profiles of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/$ C (Fig. 4b), the first discharge curve displays two distinct plateaus at $0.75{-}0.88\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.22\substack{-0.45\mathrm{V}}$ , signify the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion, the ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ reduction and the generation of SEI film, respectively. And the potential of $1.24-1.75\mathrm{~V~}$ in first charge curve indicates the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ extraction and the oxidation of Ni. Although the potential platforms of following dis/-charge curves have declined, the expressive reduction and oxidation reaction of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ are identical to that of the first dis/-charge curves, which are well consistent with the CV mechanisms.
The sodium storage capability and rate capability for nickelic spheres were investigated by the galvanostatic dis/-charge measurements. In order to deduct the capacity provided by the carbon, the test voltage was set to $0.05{-}3\mathrm{~V~}$ . The $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode (Fig. 4c) displays ultrahigh initial dis/-charge capacities $(3222.1/1979.3\$ $\operatorname{mAh}{\mathrm{g}^{-1}}.$ ) with initial Coulombic efficiency (ICE) of $61.4\%$ at 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The irreversible capacity may be caused by the decomposition of electrolyte, the generation of SEI films, the inadequate charge transfer, and/or heterogeneous interfacial storage mechanism [45]. However, this fast capacity decay is a general phenomenon existing in metal oxides, phosphides, sulfides, selenides and fluorides [46,47]. Several strategies can be taken to increase the initial CE, for example, (1) doping metal (eg. Fe, Co, Cu) or nonmetal (eg. N, S, P) atoms, (2) reducing the particle size to nanometer size, (3) wrapping with pure carbon layer outside of sphere, (4) growing with the substrate (eg. carbon cloth, carbon paper, carbon fiber).
Future work will be conducted to improve the initial CE. Due to the inadequate activation, the reversible capacity declines and finally stabilizes at $765.4\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with $98\%$ CE after 110 cycles. Benefiting from uniqueness of the yolk-shell structure, the reversible capacity of yolk-shell $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres is superior to that of hollow $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres $(628.8\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}})$ and solid $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres $528.1~\mathrm{\mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , Fig. S10). This implies yolk-shell structure plays a greater role in improving the electrochemical performance than the hollow or solid structure. In Fig. 4d, the discharge specific capacities gradually decrease and maintain 829.2, 692.4, 618.5, 502.3 and 491.6 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1 and $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and then regain 812.9 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the density returns $0.1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , which is basically consistent with the result of galvanostatic dis/-charge measurement at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . The fast charge and discharge property is also an important index to measure the battery performance.
Moreover, the kinetics and storage mechanisms of nickelic composites were studied with CV measurements at different scan rates of $0.2\mathrm{-}1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . The storage mechanisms include the faradaic from ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion, pseudo-capacitance, and non-faradaic contribution [19,34]. The CV curves of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ display the similar feature in Fig. 4e, and scan rate $(\nu)$ and specified current (i) abide the following rules [44],
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{i=~a\times\upsilon^{b}}}\\ {{\phantom{{\upsilon}}}}\\ {{\log i={\mathsf{b}}\times\log\upsilon+\log{\mathsf{a}}}}\end{array}
$$
$$
i(\nu)=\mathbf{k}_{1}\times\nu^{1/2}+\mathbf{k}_{2}\times\nu
$$
Fig. 4f shows that the capacitive contributions are $69\%$ , $72\%$ , $76\%$ $81\%$ and $86\%$ at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and $1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , respectively. The fast kinetics could facilitate the sodium storage capacity.
To verify the universality and practicability of existing MOFderived method for prepared electrode materials in SIBs, the effective sodium storage properties of the other two kinds of nickelic composites have been surveyed. For the CV curves of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ electrode (Fig. S11a), the cathodic peaks of $1.11/0.77\mathrm{~V~}$ in first lap and $_{0.91\mathrm{~V~}}$ in following laps, demonstrate the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion, the reduction of $\mathrm{Ni}S_{2}$ , and the generation of SEI film. Conversely, the anodic peaks of $1.82\mathrm{~V~}$ and $1.66\mathrm{~V~}$ mean the oxidation of metallic Ni to $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ with the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ extraction $(\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}+4\mathrm{Na}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+2\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathsf{S})$ 1 [19,27]. In the corresponding dis $/$ -charge curves (Fig. S11b), the voltage tends of $1.24-1.29\mathrm{~V}/0.32–0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ in discharge and $1.45\mathrm{-}$ $1.88{\mathrm{V}}$ in charge process separately represent the similar reduction and oxidation mechanisms, well match each dis/-charge course in CV measurement of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ electrode. In Fig. S11c, the initial discharge specific capacity of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ is $2568.9\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with ICE of $56.3\%$ , and reversible capacity maintains $706.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 110 cycles at 0.1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The discharge specific cpacities are 871.3, 639.6, 530.9, 433.8 and 345.1 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1 and 2 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , and shows good rate capability (Fig. S11d). Figure S11e displays the CV curves of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ at $0.2\mathrm{-}1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ with similar shape, and the b-values are 0.48, 0.65 and 0.57 at different reduction and oxidation peaks (Fig. S11f). The capacitive contributions are
$68\%$ , $70\%$ , $72\%$ , $77\%$ and $82\%$ at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and $1\mathrm{~mV~}s^{-1}$ (Fig. S11g and h), with the fast kinetics.
Similarly, for $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode (Fig. S12a), the cathodic peaks of $0.99/0.76\mathrm{~V~}$ in initial discharge course signify the reduction of $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ , the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion, and the generation of SEI film. The anodic peak of $2.01\mathrm{~V~}$ in first charge process denotes the oxidation of Ni, and the $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ exaction $(\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}+4\mathrm{Na}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+2\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Se})$ [7,48]. Furthermore, the cathodic (0.93 V) and anodic peaks (1.89 V) in sequential loops still contain the same conversion mechanisms compared with the first dis/-charge process. The galvanostatic dis/-charge profiles in the first three laps of $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ (Fig. S12b) show the similar reduction/oxidation reaction, respectively, which are still consistent with each cathodic/anodic peak in CV curves. For $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode (Fig. S12c), the initial dis/-charge capacities are $2507.8/1778.6\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and reversible capacity reaches 735.8 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with the $100\%$ CE. Besides, the $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ anode shows good rate capability (Fig. S12d). The contrastive reversible capacities and rate capability of reported $\mathrm{NiP_{x}}$ -based, $\mathrm{NiS_{x}}$ -based and $\mathrm{NiSe_{x}.}$ based electrodes are listed in Table S4-9, respectively. Compared with the congeneric electrode materials in previously reported literatures, the synthesized nickelic materials $(\mathsf{N i_{2}P/C},\mathsf{N i S_{2}/C}$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf C)$ in this article exhibit higher reversible capacity and batter rate performance, even at large current densities of $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , reversible capacities of nickelic materials have great advantages as a whole.
The b-values are 0.48, 0.66 and 0.55 at different reduction and oxidation peaks in CV curves with different scan rates for $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ (Fig. S12e and f). The capacitive contributions are $70\%$ , $74\%$ , $76\%$ ， $78\%$ and $84\%$ at 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and $1\mathrm{~mV~}s^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. $\mathbf{s12g}$ and h). Rapid charge and discharge performances are another important indicator for battery system. The long-time cycling performances of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C},\mathsf{N i}\mathsf{S}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ electrodes have been tested at the current density of $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ for 500 cycles. In Fig. S13, the specific discharge capacity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode is up to $586.3\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ , which is higher than that of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ (562.8 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ $\mathsf{\cdot546.3\ m A h\ g^{-1}}.$ electrodes, respectively. The high sodium storage capacities, good rate capability and fast kinetics of nickelic composites further explain the practicability of this current MOF-derived tactics for synthesizing energy materials.
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) test was implemented to further elucidate the storage mechanism of nickelic composites. The nonsymmetrical semicircular is composed of SEI film resistance $\left(R_{s}\right)$ and charge-transfer resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ in highmedium frequencies, and ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion resistance in low frequency [11,27]. In Fig. 5c, the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ $(98~\Omega)$ is smaller than that of $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ (149 X) and $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C\left(158\ \Omega\right)$ . After calculation, the electroconductivity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ is higher than that of $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ , indicating that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ has a faster charge transfer process in comparison to sulfide and selenide counterparts. The ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion coefficient $(D_{\mathrm{Na}^{+}})$ can be calculated by the following formula,
$$
D_{N a+}={\mathrm R}^{2}\mathrm{T}^{2}/2{\mathrm S}^{2}{\mathrm n}^{4}{\mathrm F}^{4}{\mathrm C}^{2}\sigma^{2}
$$
Particularly, the R is $8.314\mathrm{~J}/(\mathrm{mol}^{\ast}\mathrm{K}),$ T is the thermodynamic temperature (K), S is contact area $(\mathbf{m}^{2})$ of electrodes, n is the number of transferred electrons, F is $96485.3C/\mathrm{mol}$ , C is the concentration of the sodium electrolyte $(\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L})$ . Thus, when the above factors are constant, the $D_{\mathrm{Na+}}$ is up to Warburg factor $(\upsigma).$ , which is counted from the ratio of $Z^{\prime}/\upomega^{-1/2}(\Omega\ s^{-1/2})$ . In Fig. 5d, the $\upsigma$ -value of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/$ $\mathsf{C}\mathsf{\Gamma}(195.9\mathsf{\Omega}\Omega\mathsf{\Lambda}s^{-1/2})$ is much smaller than that of $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ $(414.6\ \Omega\ s^{-1/2})$ and $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ $(737.5\ \Omega\ s^{-1/2})$ , leading to the faster ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion in $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ sample.
To better understand the reaction kinetics of nickelic composites during dis/-charge process, the chemical diffusion coefficient for ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ was measured by the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT). The batteries were allowed to relax $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ to reach quasi-equilibrium, after each $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ discharging or charging period at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ in the first cycle (Fig. S14). The ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ion diffusion coefficient $(D_{\mathrm{Na+}})$ can be obtained according to the following equation [51]:
$$
D_{N a+}=~(4/\pi\tau)\cdot(n_{m}\cdot V_{m}/S)^{2}\cdot(\Delta E_{s}/\Delta E_{t})^{2}
$$
The good electrochemical performances of nickelic composites can be attributed to the synergistic effects, and confinement effects from hierarchical yolk-shell structure. The unique yolk-shell structure can provide large contact areas of electrode materials/electrolyte, and effective transport paths for $\mathsf{N a}^{+}/$ electrons, the shell could buffer the nanoparticles volume variation to maintain structural integrity. Meanwhile, the outer thin carbon layer not only can enhance the conductivity but also protect active material from damage and inhibit the irreversible side reactions. Moreover, the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ delivers the higher reversible capacity $(765.4\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\$ ) than that of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ $706.4~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}.$ ) and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ ( $735.8\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) conterparts. The reasons can be summarized as follows: (i) the existing multi-electron orbitals cause the good chemical properties for metal phosphide. The generated ${\sf N a}_{3}{\sf P}$ from conversion reaction of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ with ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ could convert into the $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ with high theoretical capacity of 2596 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in subsequent cycles, which may produce high storage capacity for ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ [52,53]. (ii) Metal phosphide displays the weak polarization, low discharge platforms, and small voltage gap, in comparison with their sulfide and selenide counterparts, thus facilitating rapid kinetics in deep conversion with enhanced sodium storage capacity. (iii) The Ni-P bonds are relatively easy to break in comparison with that of Ni-S and Ni-Se bonds [54]. (iv) The large specific surface areas in $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ sample can lead to the enhanced ions/electrons adsorption and transportation ability. More significantly, this research scientifically illustrates the phys-chemical relationships and the generated distinguishing impacts on electrochemical behaviors for nickelic composites.
The phases transformation, the insertion reaction of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and chemical states variation of nickelic composites were verified by the ex situ XRD and XPS analysis. The batteries were disassembled, the electrode plates were extracted and washed by dimethyl carbonate/ethanol to remove the organics and then measured directly after drying, the existing carbon carriers are clearly indicated by the peaks of $26^{\circ}$ in ex situ XRD (Fig. S15a, d and $\mathbf{g}$ ). In Fig. S15a, when the $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ anode was discharged to 0.05 V (DH), the several diffraction peaks of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ weaken, which is reduced to the Ni $(44.1^{\circ}$ , $56.2^{\circ}$ and $77.3^{\circ}\mathrm{:}$ , NaP (31.5, $38.7^{\circ}$ and $53.4^{\circ}.$ and $\mathsf{N a}_{3}\mathsf{P}(35.1^{\circ},42.4^{\circ}$ and $46.1^{\circ}$ ) in sodiation process. On the contrary, when the electrode was recharged to $3\mathrm{~V~}$ (CH), the intermediate products of sodium phosphides disappear completely, and the metallic Ni is oxidized to $\mathfrak{N i}_{2}\mathrm{P}\ (40.8^{\circ},44.6^{\circ},47.4^{\circ}$ and $54.6^{\circ}\mathrm{\cdot}$ in desodiation process, well consistent with the analysis in CV curves. This fact of forming NaP, ${\sf N a}_{3}{\sf P}$ , metallic Ni in discharge and ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ in recharge process proves that $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode has good reversible phases conversion $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}P+x N a^{+}+x e^{-}\leftrightarrow N a_{x}N i_{2}P}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNa_{x}N i_{2}P+(3-x)N a^{+}+}$ $(3{-}\mathbf{X})\mathbf{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow2\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{N}\mathbf{a}_{3}\mathbf{P})$ . Compared with the peak position of ${\mathsf{N i}}\ 2{\mathsf{p}}$ and $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ peaks in pristine curve (Fig. S15b and c), the position of these two peaks has shifted to low direction when the electrode was discharged to $_{0.05\mathrm{V}}$ , indicating of the insertion of $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ ions, the reduction of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and the oxidation of $\mathsf{P}^{4-}$ . While the peak position again shifts to high direction when it was recharged to $3\mathrm{V}$ , denoting the exsertion of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions, the oxidation of Ni and reduction of ${\mathsf{P}}^{3-}$ . Furthermore, it is suggested that the good reversibility of phases transformation for $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ electrode material during the $\mathsf{d e}/$ -sodiation process [48,55].
The reversibility of phase transformation for the sulfide and selenide counterparts was further elaborated. The peaks of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ ( $.10.9^{\circ}$ , $28.6^{\circ}$ , $30.6^{\circ}$ , $31.4^{\circ}$ , $33.3^{\circ}$ , $36.9^{\circ}$ and $46.4^{\circ}.$ , ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{5}$ $(12.5^{\circ}$ , $17.8^{\circ},21.9^{\circ}$ and $54.6^{\circ}$ ) and metallic N $\$123,41,1°$ and $77.3^{\circ}$ ) have appeared when the electrode was discharged to $_{0.05\mathrm{V}}$ , and the formation of $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ $(31.6^{\circ},35.7^{\circ},38.8^{\circ},45.1^{\circ},49.7^{\circ}$ and $53.6^{\circ}.$ ) when it was recharged to $3\mathrm{~V~}$ in cycled $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ electrode $\left(\mathsf{N i S_{2}}+\mathsf{x N a}^{+}+\right.$ $\mathrm{xe^{-}\leftrightarrow N a_{x}N i S_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{Na_{x}N i S_{2}\ +\ (4{-}x)N a^{+}\ +\ (4{-}x)e^{-}\leftrightarrow\ N i\ +\ 2N a_{2}S)}$ (Fig. S15d). Similarly, for the cycled $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf C$ the ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S e}$ $(25.3^{\circ}$ , $38.1^{\circ}$ , $44.6^{\circ}$ and $53.1^{\circ}\mathrm{.}$ ) and Ni phases $(50.6^{\circ}$ and $77.3^{\circ}\ \$ ) have appeared when it was discharged to $0.05\mathrm{~V~}$ , and then disappear again when it was recharged to $3\mathrm{~V~}$ with the formation of NiSe $(31.3^{\circ},36.1^{\circ},39.5^{\circ},44.5^{\circ},48.5^{\circ},55.7^{\circ}$ and $60.9^{\circ}\mathrm{{.}}$ ) and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}$ $(54.4^{\circ})$ $(\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}+\mathrm{xNa}^{+}+\mathrm{xe}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Na}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{NiSe}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Na_{x}N i S e_{2}}+\mathrm{(2-X)Na^{+}}+\mathrm{(2-$ $\mathrm{x)e^{-}\leftrightarrow N i S e+N a_{2}S e}$ $\mathrm{NiSe}+2\mathrm{Na}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Se}^{\prime},$ (Fig. S15g) [7]. Besides, the binding energies of Ni 2p, S 2p and Se 3d in $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ (Fig. S14e, f, h and i) shift toward the low value when the electrodes were discharged to $0.05{\mathrm{~V}}$ , and again shift to the high value which were recharged to $3\ \mathsf{V}.$ , also giving a good reversibility of phase transformation.
The structural stability of cycled nickelic composites was studied by the ex situ TEM analysis. The integrity of spherical skeleton for $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ is well maintained (Figs. S16a and S17a). The electrochemical active particles (marked by red circle in Fig. 6a) are tightly wrapped by outer carbon layers. The recycled particles have separated into many tiny nanoparticles with the average size of rough $15.8~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which can be ascribed to the continuous conversion reactions between ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and metal compounds. This leads to the fragmentation of internal particles during the sodiation and desodiation reaction, but the outer shape is remained intact, which is a common phenomenon in the majority of conversion-type electrodes [56–58]. The nano-sized electrochemical active particles exhibit large surface areas with abundant active sites, thus accelerating ions/electrons transportation to enhance electrochemical kinetics. The distinct lattice fringes of NaP (1 0 3), ${\sf N a}_{3}{\sf P}$ (200) and metallic Ni (111) with d-spacing of 0.296, 0.218 and $0.204\ \mathrm{nm}$ are clearly observed in HRTEM image (Fig. 6b), respectively, corresponding to the ex situ XRD results. HAADF-STEM image and EDS elemental mapping (Fig. 6c) further illustrate that the unbroken sphere are well maintained with the uniform dispersion of Ni, P and Na elements. Furthermore, the cycled $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ spheres still maintain the spherical skeleton (Figs. S16b, c and S17b, c), and the dissociated active nanoparticles are finely coated by the carbon layer (Fig. 6d and g). The HRTEM image of cycled $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ (Fig. 6e) shows the obvious lattice fringes with d-spacing of 0.285, 0.278, $0.229{\mathrm{~nm}}$ in $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ (4 0 4), ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{5}$ (1 0 2), Ni (0 1 0) planes, respectively, and Ni, S and Na elements are uniformly distributed on electrode surface (Fig. 5f). While the cycled $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ reveals the lattice fringes of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{S e}$ (3 1 1), (2 2 0) and Ni (0 0 2) corresponding to d-spacing of 0.206, 0.242 and $0.217~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 6h), with the uniform distribution of Ni, Se and Na elements (Fig. 6i). Therefore, these above results demonstrate that the robust spherical skeleton integrity for nickelic composites is basically maintained.
The density functional theory (DFT) calculations were applied to study the structures of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ and ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ adsorption principle on ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ . The structure and crystal structure corresponding to (1 1 1), (201) and (210) of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ are shown in Figs. S18 and S19. During the discharge process, the reduction occurs and Ni-P bonds breaks. The ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions are absorbed on the surface and form the ${\sf N a}_{\mathrm{x}}{\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}$ , the adsorption structures of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ on different lattice planes are shown in Fig. S20. To evaluate the stability of Na adsorbed on ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}$ , we have calculated the adsorption energy of a Na adatom as follows:
$$
E_{a d,d}(N a)\ =E_{N a,d}-E_{N a}-E_{d}
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{Na,d}}$ is the total energy of Na adsorbed on ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ with and $E_{\mathrm{Na}}$ is taken as the total energy of bulk Na. The adsorption energy of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ is shown in Table 1. Charge transfer is most sensible to ionic bonding and has little connection to covalent. To understand the bonding nature of Na adsorbed on ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ , the difference charge density $\Delta\uprho$ has been calculated as follows [59]:
$$
\Delta\rho=\rho_{\mathrm{Na+G}}--[\rho_{\mathrm{Na}}+\rho_{\mathrm{G}}]
$$
Where $\uprho_{\mathrm{Na+G}}$ signifies the charge density for the total system (Na and $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}.$ ), $\uprho_{\mathsf{G}}$ is the charge density of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ (without Na), and $\uprho_{\mathrm{Na}}$ is the charge density of an isolated Na atom located at the same position as in the total system. Fig. $\mathtt{7a-c}$ shows the difference charge densities of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ adsorbed on ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}$ lattice planes. In all three cases, there is a net charge gain in the middle region between Na and ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}_{:}$ whereas there is a net charge loss above the Na, indicating a significant charge transfer from the adsorbed Na to its nearest neighbor Ni atoms.
The deeper electrochemical mechanism can be summarized as follows: (i) during the discharge process, the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions insert into the nickelic composites $(\mathrm{NiX_{n}}/\mathsf{C},\mathsf{X}=\mathsf{P},\mathsf{S},$ Se), within the migration of electrons to the cathode, leading to expansion of lattice spacing. Fur-thermore, the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions are reduced to the ${\sf N i}^{0}$ and the oxidation of $\mathsf{X}^{2\mathrm{n-}}$ ions, with the formation of SEI film $\left(\mathrm{NiX_{n}}+x\mathrm{Na}^{+}+x\mathrm{e}^{-}\right.$ $\rightarrow\mathsf{N a}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{N i X}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , $\mathrm{Na_{x}N i X_{n}}+(n\mathrm{-}x)\mathrm{Na^{+}}+(2\mathrm{-}x)\mathrm{e^{-}}\rightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Na_{n}X_{n}})$ . The binding energy of Ni 2p, S $2{\tt p}$ and Se 3d slightly shifts towards low value. (ii) During the recharging process, the extraction of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions and electrons migrate to the anode. The metallic ${\mathsf{N i}}^{0}$ is oxi-dized to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions and the reduction of $\mathrm{X^{-}\left(N i+N a_{n}X_{n}-(2-}x)e^{-}\rightarrow N a_{x}N i X_{n}+\right.}$ $\left(n{-}x\right)\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Na_{x}N i X_{n}}-x e^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{NiX_{n}}+x\mathrm{Na^{+}})$ . The binding energy of Ni 2p, S 2p and Se 3d again returns to high value, indicating of the good reversibility of phases transformation for nickelic composites during the $\mathsf{d e}/$ -sodiation process.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a general metal–organic framework-derived route via Ostwald ripening to synthesize three kinds of carbon-encapsulated yolk-shell nickelic spheres $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/C$ $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf C)$ by hydrothermal and anion exchange strategy. The key progresses presented in this study compared to previous literature are described below, the phys/-chemical properties of nickel phosphate are superior to that of sulfide and selenide counterparts, including highly active phosphide phase, large BET specific surface area, weak polarization, low discharge platform, small voltage gap and fast kinetics. Therefore, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{C}$ anode delivers much high initial specific capacity $(3222.1/1979.3\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}};$ and high reversible capacity of $765.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , in comparison to the storage sodium capacities of $\mathsf{N i S}_{2}/\mathsf C$ $706.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ 1 and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C}$ $735.8\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) electrodes. Furthermore, the reversible capacities and rate capabilities of three synthesized carbonencapsulated nickelic spheres $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}P/C,N i S_{2}/C}$ and $\mathsf{N i S e}_{2}/\mathsf{C})$ by comparison are superior to similar electrode materials reported in previous literatures. This effective and novel MOF-derived approach can be extended to synthesize other carbon-combined metal compounds as energy storage materials.
